From: p5r-proton
Subject: nsiproxy: restore Wine 7 IPv6 forwarding table behaviour

Proton 7 (Wine 7) returned an empty IPv6 forwarding table immediately from
ipv6_forward_enumerate_all().  Newer Wine bases parse the whole of
/proc/net/ipv6_route on every call, including calls that only ask for the
entry count.

Persona 5 Royal's Steamworks network worker polls that table continuously.
With the modern implementation it re-reads and re-parses the host routing
table hundreds of times per second, which keeps a CPU core busy and caps the
title screen near 30 FPS.  Restore the Wine 7 fast path so the polling is
cheap again, matching the fast Proton 7 baseline.

--- a/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/ip.c
+++ b/dlls/nsiproxy.sys/ip.c
@@ -1791,6 +1791,12 @@ static NTSTATUS ipv6_forward_enumerate_all( void *key_data, UINT key_size, void *
     TRACE( "%p %d %p %d %p %d %p %d %p\n" , key_data, key_size, rw_data, rw_size,
         dynamic_data, dynamic_size, static_data, static_size, count );
 
+    /* Wine 7 returned an empty IPv6 forwarding table without parsing host
+     * routes.  P5R polls this table from a network worker thread, so do the
+     * same here instead of re-reading /proc/net/ipv6_route on every call. */
+    *count = 0;
+    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+
 #ifdef __linux__
     {
         struct ipv6_route_data entry;
